Input: {"question": "What political party was Daniel Hogan affiliated with?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 0, "end": 107, "text": "He was at many of Grant's major battles, including Fort Henry, Fort Donelson, Corinth, Nashville, and Iuka."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 108, "end": 224, "text": "He was mustered out at the close of the war, whereupon he attended the business school at Bryant & Stratton College."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 225, "end": 289, "text": "After graduation, he worked for Western Union in several cities."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 290, "end": 365, "text": "He returned to Mound City, Illinois in 1869 to attend to his aging parents."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 366, "end": 449, "text": "In 1873, Hogan was elected Pulaski County Clerk, holding the office for nine years."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 450, "end": 494, "text": "Hogan purchased the Pulaski Patriot in 1880."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 495, "end": 561, "text": "In 1882, Hogan was elected as a Republican to the Illinois Senate."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d00", "start": 562, "end": 587, "text": "He was re-elected in each"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d01", "start": 0, "end": 17, "text": "and Republicans ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d01", "start": 18, "end": 51, "text": "It ’ s more important than that ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0091-4b6bfd5926d4:d01", "start": 52, "end": 142, "text": "This is a fight for Maryland and